#===============================================================================
# PROJECT REYES -- UNIFIED PIPELINE
# Objective: Assess whether KDPI and LDRI affect outcomes in KALT (kidney after
#            liver transplant, ANY interval) vs. true liver-transplant-alone
#            (LTA) patients who developed ESRD.
#
#            KALT is ONE category: the <1-year and >=1-year timing strata are
#            both KALT, and "LTA" means never received a kidney. The exposure
#            is defined in exactly one place -- KALT_MAX_DAYS ([K1], Sec 1.3).
#            Because a >=1-year recipient is unexposed for a median 4.0 years
#            first, every survival clock anchored at liver transplant treats
#            the exposure as TIME-VARYING; see [K2].
#
# This file MERGES:
#   (1) the cohort-construction / Table 1-3 script, and
#   (2) the survival-endpoints script
# into a single top-to-bottom run.
#
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THE MERGE FIXES THE KDPI-TIER n DISCREPANCY
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# In the split version, kidpan_data.sas7bdat was read and de-duplicated THREE
# separate times with three different column sets (KID_COLS, KID_COLS_TABLE3,
# KID_COMPOSITE_COLS). Each read produced its own slice(1) winner per patient.
# Because the tie-breaks operate on columns that differ between the reads, a
# patient could get a different kidney registration row -- and therefore a
# different KDPI -- in Table 2 vs. Table 3 vs. the survival analysis.
#
# Here the file is read ONCE, de-duplicated ONCE, and every downstream table
# and model keys off that single frame. Tier counts are now reconcilable by
# construction (Section 12 proves it).
#
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUBSTANTIVE CHANGES YOU MUST REVIEW BEFORE TRUSTING OUTPUT
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [C1] The `!is.na(KDPI)` filter has MOVED. It used to sit in the cohort
#      definition (df_filterEC_kidpan_main), where it silently required every
#      cohort member to have received a deceased-donor kidney -- which made the
#      LTA arm structurally impossible to populate with never-transplanted
#      patients. KDPI is now filtered at POINT OF USE (Tables 2/3, KDPI-tiered
#      survival). Set RESTRICT_COHORT_TO_KDPI <- TRUE to reproduce old numbers.
#
# [C2] Living-donor kidney recipients now enter the cohort (they were removed
#      by the old KDPI filter as a side effect). Controlled by KIDNEY_DON_TY_KEEP.
#
# [C3] The duplicated Table 2 block (it appeared twice, verbatim) is gone.
#
# [C4] LITYP reference coding is contradictory in the old script -- the comment
#      says "LITYP == 1 is Whole" but the code treats 0 as whole and 1:21 as
#      split. This is now a single named constant, LITYP_WHOLE_CODES.
#      RESOLVED: the old CODE was right and the old COMMENT was wrong. Verified
#      against star-file-data-dictionary.xlsx (LIVER_DATA -> format
#      ALL_LISPLITTYPE): 0 = Whole Liver, 1-21 = partial/split variants,
#      **OTHER** = Unknown. Empirically 0 covers 92.6% of non-missing LITYP,
#      which is what an adult LT registry should look like. Do NOT "fix" this
#      back toward the old comment. Section 9 now asserts it at run time.
#
# [C5] as.Date(ifelse(...)) in the survival endpoint builders stripped the Date
#      class. Replaced with class-preserving indexing.
#
# [C6] Time-to-ESRD now uses the patient's EARLIEST kidney listing across all
#      registrations, not the INIT_DATE that happened to ride along on the
#      de-duplicated row. Old behaviour available via ESRD_DATE_SOURCE.
#===============================================================================


