#            Table 2 totals exactly.
#   "all"  = every deceased-donor kidney recipient in the cohort (KALT + LTA).
#            Broader donor-characteristics picture, but its tier n will then
#            exceed Table 3's KALT-only tier n by exactly the LTA count in
#            each tier (see Section 11 [4] reconciliation).
#
# [K1] THE TWO SETTINGS NOW COINCIDE. Under KALT_MAX_DAYS = Inf every
# deceased-donor kidney recipient in the cohort IS a KALT recipient, so "KALT"
# and "all" select the same rows and Section 11 [4]'s "LTA only" column is
# empty by construction. The switch is kept because it becomes load-bearing
# again the moment KALT_MAX_DAYS is narrowed.
#
# CONSEQUENCE FOR THE REFERENCE-MANUSCRIPT CHECK: the 107/624/71 deceased and
# 313 LD-KALT tier counts quoted above were computed under the OLD <365-day
# definition. Widening KALT to any interval adds the >=1-year recipients to
# every tier, so Tables 2 and 3 will NO LONGER reproduce those numbers, and
# Section 12's cross-checks will report the difference. That is the intended
# effect of the definition change, not a regression -- set KALT_MAX_DAYS <- 365
# to reproduce the reference Ns.
TABLE2_POPULATION <- "KALT"

