#     same underlying alcohol-related disease spectrum. If your co-authors
#     want only 4215 counted, narrow this list.
#   - "NASH" folds in 4221 (MASH -- metabolic dysfunction-associated
#     steatohepatitis), the renamed/updated term for NASH in newer SAF
#     releases, alongside 4214 (the original "FATTY LIVER (NASH)" code).
#   - 4222 (METALD -- metabolic dysfunction AND alcohol-related disease) and
#     4206/4210/4200/4201/4203/4205/4208/4209/4212/4213 do not fit any of the
#     reference table's eight categories and fall to "Other/unmapped" by
#     design -- these did not exist, or were not distinguished, when the
#     source manuscript's classification was likely built.
TABLE7_ALCOHOLIC_CIRRHOSIS_CODES <- c(4215, 4217, 4218, 4219)
TABLE7_HCV_CIRRHOSIS_CODES       <- c(4204, 4593)
TABLE7_HCV_ALCOHOLIC_COMBO_CODES <- c(4216)
TABLE7_NASH_CODES                <- c(4214, 4221)

# Minimum events a post-KALT interval (Block 2) needs before it is fit as its
# own term. Death-censored liver graft failure is typically far rarer than
# death, so the same 8-interval split that works for patient survival can
# easily leave several liver-graft intervals with zero events -- which does
# not just produce a wide confidence interval, it makes the Cox partial
# likelihood MONOTONE: the MLE for that term does not exist and coxph reports
# a coefficient that has diverged to +-Inf (an absurd HR like 3e7, or exactly
# 0, with a p-value near 1 and a degenerate CI extending to 0 or Inf -- NOT a
# real estimate, and not fixable by rounding/formatting). Section 24.4 checks
# every interval's event count against this threshold BEFORE fitting and
# merges any interval that falls short into its temporal neighbor, endpoint by
# endpoint, so patient survival (usually well powered) keeps the full 8
# intervals while liver graft survival (usually sparser) gets an automatically
# coarser, but estimable, set. Every merge is printed and footnoted -- nothing
# is silently changed.
TABLE4_MIN_EVENTS_PER_INTERVAL <- 5

