# [F7] Every curve in this section is stratified on Group3, so the true-LTA
# patients and the two KALT timing strata appear as separate curves rather than
# being averaged together. The log-rank tests are correspondingly 3-group
# (2 df); pairwise comparisons are printed in 18.3b so the specific
# KALT-vs-each contrast is still available.
#
# *** READ THIS BEFORE INTERPRETING THE "KALT (>=1 y)" CURVE ***
# [K2] These are FIXED-LABEL curves on a liver-TX clock, which is exactly the
# construction Figures 7-9 avoid by restricting to KALT <1 y ([K3]).
# Separating the strata makes their labels
# honest, but it does NOT make the >=1-year curve a treatment effect -- it makes
# the immortal-time artifact visible instead of hidden. A patient can only be
# classified "KALT (>=1 y)" by surviving at least 365 days past their liver
# transplant AND long enough to actually receive a kidney (median 4.0 years
# post-transplant). So that curve is
# guaranteed to sit at or near 100% survival through the first year on either
# anchor -- in this cohort it has literally 0 deaths by day 365 from liver
# listing -- and its apparent advantage over both other groups (pairwise
# p < 2e-16 below) is largely definitional, not clinical.
#
# The unbiased version of that comparison is the time-varying model in Section
# 24 Block 2, where those pre-transplant years are credited to the untransplanted
# reference state (TABLE4_LATE_TX_TIME_VARYING = TRUE, Section 1.3c [F6]).
# Section 25's Table 5 shows the same contrast both ways: a 30-point fixed-label
# KM gap that becomes HR 0.99 once the clock is handled correctly.
# These curves are DESCRIPTIVE. Do not quote them as effects.

#--- 18.1 Patient survival by group, transplant-anchored ----------------------
# NOTE: this anchor lets immortal time into any comparison involving a
