#===============================================================================
# SECTION 24. TABLE 4 -- Survival Benefit of KALT: Time-Varying and
#             Propensity/Covariate-Adjusted Hazard Ratios
#===============================================================================
# Reproduces the reference "Table 4. Survival Benefit of KALT: Hazard Ratios
# from Time-varying mortality hazards and Propensity Score Models," three
# blocks, each reported for patient survival and death-censored liver graft
# survival:
#
#   Block 1 -- HRs of LDRI 1.1-1.6 and LDRI >1.6 relative to LDRI <=1.1, among
#              ALL waitlisted kidney-after-liver candidates (LTA + KALT
#              combined; per the reference footnote, "all waitlisted KALT
#              candidates" = everyone who was ever a candidate for a kidney
#              after their liver TX, which is this pipeline's whole
#              df_esrd_cohort). Ordinary (non-time-varying) multivariable Cox
#              model, anchored at kidney listing (reuses Section 23's
#              df_supp1_all endpoint construction -- see [F3] there).
#
#   Block 2 -- Post-KALT TIME-VARYING HRs for being transplanted, relative to
#              remaining on the waiting list/dialysis, split into intervals
#              since the KALT transplant. Built via survival::tmerge()
#              counting-process expansion: every patient starts in the
#              "Waitlist/dialysis" state at kidney listing; a KALT patient's
#              covariate switches at their kidney TX date and is then further
#              split at the interval cutpoints in TABLE4_CUT_DAYS (Section
#              1.3c). This is the standard way to avoid immortal time bias
#              when the "exposure" (transplantation) can only happen partway
#              through follow-up -- see the extensive discussion of this in
#              Sections 18/19.
#
#   Block 3 -- A single summary HR for LTA relative to KALT (Group as a FIXED,
#              not time-varying, covariate -- see TABLE4_LATE_TX_TIME_VARYING
#              in Section 1.3c for why this is deliberately consistent with
#              Group elsewhere in the pipeline rather than with Block 2's
#              time-varying definition). Reference table titles this row
#              "Propensity Score-adjusted"; footnote (a) instead describes
#              direct multivariable covariate adjustment. This code implements
#              the footnote's literal covariate list (transparent, fully
#              reproducible from df_esrd_cohort); Section 24.6 shows the
#              alternative -- adjusting for the Section 21 propensity score as
#              a single covariate -- commented out, in case your co-authors
#              specifically want that operationalization.
#
# All three blocks adjust for: age, ethnicity, sex, LDRI (except Block 1,
# where LDRI is the exposure), hypertension, diabetes, dialysis, cause of
# liver failure, and cause of ESRD -- per the reference table's footnote (a).
#
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ON "NE" (NOT ESTIMABLE) AND MERGED INTERVALS IN THIS TABLE
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Death-censored liver graft failure is typically far rarer than death. Once
# that rare event is split across several covariate levels (Block 1/3) or
# several narrow post-transplant time windows (Block 2), it is common for some
# cell to have exactly ZERO events. When that happens the Cox partial
# likelihood is MONOTONE: there is no finite maximum-likelihood estimate for
# that term, and coxph does not fail -- it reports whatever coefficient it
# reached before declaring convergence, which has actually diverged towards
# +-Inf. This shows up as a wildly large or exactly-zero "hazard ratio" with a
# p-value near 1 and a confidence interval touching 0 or Inf. That is not a
# real estimate, and no amount of rounding or reformatting makes it one.
#
# This section handles the two places that can happen differently, because
# the right fix is different in each case:
#   - Block 1 / Block 3 (fixed categorical exposure): a zero-event LEVEL is a
#     property of the data, not of an arbitrary time grid, so the honest fix
#     is to report that specific term as "NE" and print the event count that
#     caused it (Sections 24.3/24.6), rather than inventing a different
#     grouping that was not part of the table's design.
#   - Block 2 (time-varying intervals): the interval boundaries in
#     TABLE4_CUT_DAYS ARE an arbitrary choice, so when an interval has too few
#     events to support its own coefficient, Section 24.4 automatically merges
#     it with its temporal neighbor (separately for each endpoint, since
#     patient survival and liver graft survival can need different
#     granularity) and reports the coarser, but estimable, window instead.
#     Every merge is printed; nothing is silently changed.
# A residual safety net (fit_coxph_checked, Section 2) still catches and
# labels "NE" any term that separates for a reason other than the above.
#===============================================================================

#--- 24.1 Shared adjustment covariate list ------------------------------------

# [K2] REFUSED COMBINATION. A merged KALT arm (KALT_MAX_DAYS = Inf) fitted with
# a FIXED post-transplant label (TABLE4_LATE_TX_TIME_VARYING = FALSE) is the one
# configuration that reproduces the documented artifact: the >=1-year
# recipients' median 4.0 pre-transplant years get credited to the exposed state,
# ~8,385 event-free person-years land in the KALT arm, and what the old
# definition showed as a null (p=0.19) becomes a 14.5-point "benefit"
# (p<0.001) -- with the matched patient-survival HR moving from 0.71-0.81
# (CIs crossing 1) to 0.29 (0.24-0.37). Nothing downstream would flag it --
# the model converges and the table renders. Stop here instead.
if (is.infinite(KALT_MAX_DAYS) && !isTRUE(TABLE4_LATE_TX_TIME_VARYING)) {
  stop("[Sec 24.1] KALT_MAX_DAYS = Inf requires TABLE4_LATE_TX_TIME_VARYING = TRUE.\n",
       "  With KALT merged across both timing strata, a fixed post-transplant\n",
       "  label puts >=1-year recipients' pre-kidney person-time into the exposed\n",
       "  state and manufactures a survival benefit. See [F6]/[K2] in Section 1.3.\n",
       "  Either set TABLE4_LATE_TX_TIME_VARYING <- TRUE, or narrow\n",
       "  KALT_MAX_DAYS <- 365 to restore the old two-arm definition.")
}

